WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.522
Elmi Guray Academy.

2
00:00:02.472 --> 00:00:04.578
Lesson 6.

3
00:00:04.578 --> 00:00:08.546
Prompt and Context Engineering Recommendations.

4
00:00:08.716 --> 00:00:24.209
Lesson Overview Course AI Tools for Developers Lesson Lesson 6 Topic Prompt and Context Engineering Level Beginner Learning Objectives By the end of this lesson, you should be able to: Write prompts that are clear, concise, and precise.

5
00:00:24.159 --> 00:00:28.697
Separate useful context from unnecessary information.

6
00:00:28.697 --> 00:00:31.876
Think and plan before sending a prompt.

7
00:00:31.876 --> 00:00:36.455
Warn the AI about known difficulties and common mistakes.

8
00:00:36.455 --> 00:00:40.857
Tell an AI agent which tools or features it should use.

9
00:00:40.857 --> 00:00:47.121
Understand that the developer remains responsible for directing and reviewing the AI.

10
00:00:47.121 --> 00:00:52.474
Main principle: AI does not remove the developer from the process.

11
00:00:52.474 --> 00:00:59.689
The developer defines the goal, supplies the right context, sets the limits, and checks the result.

12
00:00:59.689 --> 00:01:08.276
English Technical Section Prompt engineering is the practice of writing instructions that clearly describe what an AI system should do.

13
00:01:08.276 --> 00:01:19.594
Context engineering is the practice of supplying the information, files, rules, examples, and constraints that the AI needs to perform the task correctly.

14
00:01:19.594 --> 00:01:24.390
A strong result normally requires both: A clear instruction.

15
00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:27.325
Relevant supporting information.

16
00:01:27.325 --> 00:01:36.768
1 Be Concise and Precise A prompt should contain enough detail to define the task, but it should not be filled with unrelated explanations.

17
00:01:36.768 --> 00:01:39.798
Concise does not mean incomplete.

18
00:01:39.798 --> 00:01:44.621
It means that every sentence should help the AI understand the goal.

19
00:01:44.621 --> 00:01:49.539
A precise prompt usually identifies: The exact task.

20
00:01:49.539 --> 00:01:52.447
The part of the project involved.

21
00:01:52.447 --> 00:01:54.662
Important limits.

22
00:01:54.662 --> 00:01:57.080
The expected output.

23
00:01:57.080 --> 00:01:59.716
How success should be checked.

24
00:01:59.716 --> 00:02:08.466
Very long prompts can still be unclear when they contain repeated ideas, unnecessary history, or unrelated details.

25
00:02:08.466 --> 00:02:16.061
2 Avoid Unnecessary Context Context is useful only when it helps the AI complete the current task.

26
00:02:16.061 --> 00:02:21.971
Do not attach or reference a file simply because you suspect that it might be related.

27
00:02:21.971 --> 00:02:28.711
Include files that you know contain relevant code, settings, requirements, or examples.

28
00:02:28.711 --> 00:02:33.711
Useful context may include: The file where the problem occurs.

29
00:02:33.711 --> 00:02:36.700
A related configuration file.

30
00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:39.336
The exact error message.

31
00:02:39.336 --> 00:02:42.569
A required design or business rule.

32
00:02:42.569 --> 00:02:45.681
An example of the expected result.

33
00:02:45.681 --> 00:02:50.871
Unnecessary context may include: Unrelated project files.

34
00:02:50.871 --> 00:02:54.268
Old instructions that no longer apply.

35
00:02:54.268 --> 00:02:58.507
Long documentation pages unrelated to the task.

36
00:02:58.507 --> 00:03:02.189
Personal stories that do not affect the solution.

37
00:03:02.189 --> 00:03:11.414
3 Think, Plan, Then Prompt Before sending a request to an AI tool, pause and identify what you actually want to achieve.

38
00:03:11.414 --> 00:03:15.654
For a larger task, prepare a small plan first.

39
00:03:15.654 --> 00:03:22.624
This reduces repeated clarification and prevents the AI from making broad changes too early.

40
00:03:22.624 --> 00:03:24.743
Define the problem.

41
00:03:24.743 --> 00:03:28.344
Identify the files or systems involved.

42
00:03:28.344 --> 00:03:31.143
Decide what must not change.

43
00:03:31.143 --> 00:03:34.268
Describe the desired final result.

44
00:03:34.268 --> 00:03:36.849
Write and review the prompt.

45
00:03:36.849 --> 00:03:46.048
Planning mode is especially useful for tasks that involve several files, complex logic, unfamiliar code, or important data.

46
00:03:46.048 --> 00:03:54.838
4 Do Not Hide Known Challenges Do not deliberately withhold information just to see whether the AI can discover the problem by itself.

47
00:03:54.838 --> 00:04:05.382
When you already know about a dangerous area, common mistake, previous failure, or special requirement, include that information in the original prompt.

48
00:04:05.382 --> 00:04:11.659
For example: The registration form previously created duplicate student records.

49
00:04:11.659 --> 00:04:15.300
Keep the existing duplicate-checking logic.

50
00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:19.865
Do not remove or bypass it when adding the new language field.

51
00:04:19.865 --> 00:04:26.428
This gives the AI a better chance of completing the task without reintroducing an old problem.

52
00:04:26.428 --> 00:04:33.887
5 Name the Tools the AI Should Use AI coding agents may have access to several tools and features.

53
00:04:33.887 --> 00:04:44.947
These can include terminal commands, search, web access, testing tools, project search, connected servers, skills, or specialized subagents.

54
00:04:44.947 --> 00:04:49.499
When a specific tool is necessary, mention it directly.

55
00:04:49.499 --> 00:04:53.439
Do not assume the agent will automatically choose it.

56
00:04:53.439 --> 00:04:59.268
Example: Use the terminal to run the existing test command after the change.

57
00:04:59.268 --> 00:05:02.162
Do not install new packages.

58
00:05:02.162 --> 00:05:07.692
If a test fails, report the exact error before editing another file.

59
00:05:07.692 --> 00:05:15.450
This instruction tells the agent which tool to use, what not to do, and how to respond if a problem appears.

60
00:05:15.450 --> 00:05:23.982
6 You Remain in Control The AI may suggest code, edit files, run commands, analyze errors, or

61
00:05:24.172 --> 00:05:26.350
review changes.

62
00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:31.762
However, the developer remains responsible for the direction of the work.

63
00:05:31.762 --> 00:05:35.879
You should decide: What the AI is allowed to change.

64
00:05:35.879 --> 00:05:38.542
Which files it may inspect.

65
00:05:38.542 --> 00:05:41.082
Which tools it should use.

66
00:05:41.082 --> 00:05:43.800
How the result should be tested.

67
00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:47.074
Whether the final output is acceptable.

68
00:05:47.074 --> 00:05:49.751
The AI is an assistant.

69
00:05:49.751 --> 00:05:52.740
The developer is the decision-maker.

70
00:05:52.740 --> 00:06:01.816
Recommended Prompt Structure A practical developer prompt can use the following structure: Task: Describe exactly what should be done.

71
00:06:01.816 --> 00:06:07.754
Relevant context: List the files, error message, or rules that matter.

72
00:06:07.754 --> 00:06:11.490
Constraints: State what must not be changed.

73
00:06:11.490 --> 00:06:16.762
Tools: Name any tool, command, or feature that should be used.

74
00:06:16.762 --> 00:06:21.150
Verification: Explain how the result should be checked.

75
00:06:21.150 --> 00:06:26.137
Output: State what the AI should return after completing the work.

76
00:06:26.137 --> 00:06:33.202
Complete Example Task: Add a required preferred-language field to the student registration page.

77
00:06:33.202 --> 00:06:40.226
Relevant context: The page is in src/app/register.tsx.

78
00:06:40.226 --> 00:06:44.167
The students table already has a language column.

79
00:06:44.167 --> 00:06:47.455
The choices must be English and Somali.

80
00:06:47.455 --> 00:06:51.857
Constraints: Keep the existing duplicate-student check.

81
00:06:51.857 --> 00:06:54.751
Do not change payment logic.

82
00:06:54.751 --> 00:06:57.917
Do not edit unrelated files.

83
00:06:57.917 --> 00:07:00.919
Keep the existing page design.

84
00:07:00.919 --> 00:07:06.531
Tools: Search the project for the existing registration insert function.

85
00:07:06.531 --> 00:07:10.471
Run the existing type-check command after editing.

86
00:07:10.471 --> 00:07:17.088
Verification: Confirm that registration cannot be submitted without selecting a language.

87
00:07:17.088 --> 00:07:20.607
Confirm that the selected language is saved.

88
00:07:20.607 --> 00:07:27.767
Output: Return the complete replacement register.tsx file and list the checks performed.

89
00:07:27.767 --> 00:07:32.400
This prompt is detailed, but every part supports the task.

90
00:07:32.400 --> 00:07:36.531
It does not include unrelated background information.

